<Layout
  title="Hono Has 35M Weekly Downloads and One npm Publisher — Commit"
  description="Hono is one of the hottest web frameworks in the JavaScript ecosystem. Fast, TypeScript-first, everywhere on Cloudflare Workers. Also: a single npm publisher. Here's what that means for your production stack."
  canonical="https://getcommit.dev/blog/hono-critical/"
  publishDate="2026-04-21"
>

  <!-- Nav -->
  <nav>
    <div class="container nav-inner">
      <a href="/" class="nav-brand">Commit</a>
      <ul class="nav-links">
        <li><a href="/extension">Extension</a></li>
        <li><a href="/blog">Writing</a></li>
        <li><a href="/pricing">Pricing</a></li><li><a href="/docs">Docs</a></li><li><a href="https://github.com/piiiico/proof-of-commitment" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GitHub</a></li>
        <li><a href="mailto:pico@amdal.dev">Contact</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </nav>

  <!-- Essay -->
  <article class="essay">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="editorial">

        <!-- Header -->
        <header class="essay-header">
          <div class="essay-meta animate-in delay-1">
            <time datetime="2026-04-21">April 21, 2026</time>
          </div>
          <h1 class="essay-title animate-in delay-2">Hono Has 35M Weekly Downloads and One npm Publisher</h1>
          <p class="essay-subtitle animate-in delay-3">
            Hono is one of the hottest web frameworks in the JavaScript ecosystem &mdash; fast, TypeScript-first,
            everywhere on Cloudflare Workers and Bun. It&rsquo;s also a single point of failure for millions of
            production environments. Here&rsquo;s what the behavioral data shows.
          </p>
          <p class="essay-byline animate-in delay-4">H&aring;kon &Aring;mdal</p>
        </header>

        <!-- Body -->
        <div class="essay-body">

          <p>
            Hono is everywhere right now. If you&rsquo;re building on Cloudflare Workers, Bun, or Deno &mdash;
            you&rsquo;ve almost certainly used it. Fast, lightweight, TypeScript-first. 35 million weekly downloads.
            A GitHub star count in the tens of thousands.
          </p>
          <p>
            And a single npm publisher.
          </p>
          <p>
            I ran <a href="/package/hono/">hono</a> through <a href="https://github.com/piiiico/proof-of-commitment">proof-of-commitment</a>,
            a supply chain risk scorer that evaluates npm packages on behavioral signals &mdash; the kind of
            structural data that <code>npm audit</code> doesn&rsquo;t check.
          </p>

          <pre><code>npx proof-of-commitment hono</code></pre>

          <pre><code>Package   Risk            Score  Publishers  Downloads    Age
&#x2500;&#x2500;&#x2500;&#x2500;&#x2500;&#x2500;&#x2500;&#x2500;&#x2500;&#x2500;&#x2500;&#x2500;&#x2500;&#x2500;&#x2500;&#x2500;&#x2500;&#x2500;&#x2500;&#x2500;&#x2500;&#x2500;&#x2500;&#x2500;&#x2500;&#x2500;&#x2500;&#x2500;&#x2500;&#x2500;&#x2500;&#x2500;&#x2500;&#x2500;&#x2500;&#x2500;&#x2500;&#x2500;&#x2500;&#x2500;&#x2500;&#x2500;&#x2500;&#x2500;&#x2500;&#x2500;&#x2500;&#x2500;&#x2500;&#x2500;&#x2500;&#x2500;&#x2500;&#x2500;&#x2500;&#x2500;
hono      &#x1F534; CRITICAL     82     1           35.1M/wk     4.3y
  &#x2514; longevity=20 momentum=25 releases=20 publishers=4 github=13</code></pre>

          <p>
            Score of 82 out of 100. One npm publisher. 35 million weekly downloads per week.
          </p>
          <p>
            The result: <strong>CRITICAL</strong>.
          </p>

          <h2>What CRITICAL means</h2>

          <p>
            CRITICAL = sole publisher + &gt;10M weekly downloads. That&rsquo;s the structural profile of
            a high-value attack target &mdash; not because hono is poorly maintained (it isn&rsquo;t), but
            because it&rsquo;s concentrated risk: one person holds the npm publish key for a package that
            runs in millions of production environments.
          </p>
          <p>
            This is the same structural profile behind the ua-parser-js attack (October 2021):
            one npm token compromised, malicious versions published, used by Facebook, Microsoft, and Google.
            <code>npm audit</code> showed zero issues beforehand. Behavioral scoring had the structural flag
            for years: sole maintainer, millions of downloads. The same profile applies to hono today.
          </p>
          <p>
            The tool doesn&rsquo;t predict that hono <em>will</em> be attacked. It identifies the conditions
            that make it a high-value target.
          </p>

          <h2>The score breakdown</h2>

          <p>The breakdown tells a more nuanced story:</p>

          <ul>
            <li><strong>Longevity: 20/25</strong> &mdash; 4.3 years, solid but not ancient</li>
            <li><strong>Download momentum: 25/25</strong> &mdash; 35M/week and growing fast (full marks)</li>
            <li><strong>Release consistency: 20/20</strong> &mdash; actively maintained, regular releases</li>
            <li><strong>Publisher depth: 4/15</strong> &mdash; this is where it falls apart</li>
            <li><strong>GitHub backing: 13/15</strong> &mdash; strong</li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            The package is well-maintained by yusukebe. It has excellent release cadence. The risk
            isn&rsquo;t abandonment &mdash; it&rsquo;s concentration. Everything that makes hono popular
            makes it a concentrated attack surface.
          </p>

          <h2>What this looks like in your project</h2>

          <p>
            If you have a <code>package.json</code> that depends on hono, run:
          </p>

          <pre><code>npx proof-of-commitment --file package.json</code></pre>

          <p>
            Or drop your <code>package.json</code> at <a href="https://getcommit.dev/audit">getcommit.dev/audit</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            You&rsquo;ll likely see <a href="/package/hono/">hono</a> flagged CRITICAL alongside <a href="/package/zod/">zod</a> (160M downloads/week, 1 publisher)
            and possibly <a href="/package/chalk/">chalk</a> (424M/week, 1 publisher). Three packages that power most modern TypeScript projects.
          </p>

          <h2>What to do with this</h2>

          <p>
            Nothing immediately needs to change. Hono is a great framework. yusukebe is an active maintainer. But:
          </p>

          <ol>
            <li>
              <strong>Know your concentrated risk.</strong> When you have sole-publisher packages at 10M+
              downloads/week in your stack, that&rsquo;s a dependency that warrants extra attention on
              security advisories.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Watch for unusual releases.</strong> The ua-parser-js attack was a malicious publish &mdash;
              a new version that wasn&rsquo;t expected. Having awareness that a package is high-concentration
              means you pay closer attention when something unusual appears.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Consider it in your threat model.</strong> CI/CD pipelines that auto-update dependencies
              are particularly exposed to this class of attack.
            </li>
          </ol>

          <p>
            The tool doesn&rsquo;t tell you to stop using hono. It tells you that hono is structurally
            similar to axios &mdash; and that&rsquo;s worth knowing.
          </p>

          <hr style="margin: 3rem 0; border: none; border-top: 1px solid var(--border);" />

          <p style="font-size: 0.875rem; color: var(--ink-muted);">
            <a href="https://github.com/piiiico/proof-of-commitment">proof-of-commitment</a> is a
            zero-install CLI, GitHub Action, and MCP server for supply chain risk scoring.
            <a href="https://getcommit.dev/audit?packages=hono,zod,chalk,axios,express">Try it live</a>
            &mdash; pre-loaded with common packages, auto-runs on arrival.
          </p>

        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>

